Keystone Security Statement

Effective Date: 04/09/2026

1. Overview

Keystone is a Jira-integrated application built on Atlassian Forge. This document describes how Keystone handles data security, storage, and access.

2. Hosting and Infrastructure

Keystone is built and hosted entirely on Atlassian Forge.

All application logic runs within Atlassian-managed infrastructure
Data is stored using Atlassian-hosted storage (Forge storage)
Keystone does not operate or rely on external servers
3. Data Storage and Processing

Keystone stores data only as required to provide application functionality.

This may include:

Atlassian account identifiers (such as accountId)
User configuration and preferences
Team and administrative configuration data
Application-generated content (such as meeting notes, summaries, or feed items)

All data is stored within Atlassian infrastructure and is not transferred to external systems.

4. Data Access and Authentication
Authentication is managed entirely by Atlassian
Keystone uses Atlassian’s authorization model (asUser() and asApp() APIs)
Keystone does not store or manage user credentials

Access to data is restricted based on:

Jira permissions
Atlassian account access controls
5. Data Transmission

All data access occurs through secure Atlassian APIs.

Data is transmitted over HTTPS
No direct external API calls are made to third-party systems
6. Third-Party Services

Keystone does not use:

External databases
Third-party analytics tools
External data processors
External authentication providers
7. Data Retention and Deletion
Data stored by Keystone is retained only as long as necessary for functionality
When the app is uninstalled, stored data is removed in accordance with Atlassian Forge platform behavior
8. Logging and Monitoring

Keystone uses standard application logging within Atlassian infrastructure.

Logs may include limited identifiers (such as accountId) for operational purposes
Logs are not used for profiling or external analysis
9. Data Export

Keystone may allow users to export or download data (such as release notes).

Exported data is initiated by the user
Exported files are stored on the user’s local device
Keystone does not track or store exported files
10. Security Responsibility Model

Keystone follows a shared responsibility model:

Atlassian is responsible for infrastructure, hosting, and platform security
Keystone is responsible for application-level data handling and access logic
Customers are responsible for user permissions and data within their Jira environment
11. Security Updates

Keystone is actively maintained and updated to:

Address bugs and vulnerabilities
Improve performance and reliability
Align with Atlassian platform security standards
